# PWD Record Update — External API Integration Guide

> **Version:** 2.0  
> **Date:** 2026-03-13  
> **Audience:** External operator (ministry / provincial department)  

---

## Overview

This API allows external operators to **update the status** of disability (PWD) records using the record's unique `SPID` and `disability_code`.

You will be provided a dedicated Keycloak account for API access.

---

## 1. Credentials

You will receive the following from the SPR team:

| Credential        | Description                              | Example                          |
|--------------------|------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|
| `KEYCLOAK_URL`     | Keycloak token endpoint                  | `https://auth.example.com/realms/spr/protocol/openid-connect/token` |
| `BASE_URL`         | API base URL                             | `https://api.example.com`        |
| `CLIENT_ID`        | OAuth2 client ID                         | `spr-gateway-external`            |
| `CLIENT_SECRET`    | OAuth2 client secret                     | _(provided securely)_            |
| `USERNAME`         | Service account username                 | `pwd_external_user`              |
| `PASSWORD`         | Service account password                 | _(provided securely)_            |

> **Security**  
> - Store all credentials in environment variables or a secrets vault.  
> - Never expose them in client-side code, logs, or version control.  
> - Contact SPR immediately if credentials are compromised.

---

## 2. Authentication

### 2.1 Obtain Access Token

Every API call requires a **Bearer token** from Keycloak.

**Request:**

```
POST {KEYCLOAK_URL}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
```

| Field           | Value              |
|-----------------|--------------------|
| `grant_type`    | `password`         |
| `client_id`     | Your CLIENT_ID     |
| `client_secret` | Your CLIENT_SECRET |
| `username`      | Your USERNAME      |
| `password`      | Your PASSWORD      |

**cURL example:**

```bash
curl -s -X POST "${KEYCLOAK_URL}" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -d "grant_type=password" \
  -d "client_id=${CLIENT_ID}" \
  -d "client_secret=${CLIENT_SECRET}" \
  -d "username=${USERNAME}" \
  -d "password=${PASSWORD}"
```

**Response — `200 OK`:**

```json
{
  "access_token": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...",
  "expires_in": 300,
  "refresh_expires_in": 1800,
  "refresh_token": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...",
  "token_type": "Bearer"
}
```

### 2.2 Use the Token

```
Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
```

### 2.3 Token Lifecycle

| Token          | TTL      | When expired                                |
|----------------|----------|---------------------------------------------|
| Access token   | ~5 min   | Refresh using the refresh token (see below) |
| Refresh token  | ~30 min  | Re-authenticate with username / password    |

### 2.4 Refresh an Expired Token

```bash
curl -s -X POST "${KEYCLOAK_URL}" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -d "grant_type=refresh_token" \
  -d "client_id=${CLIENT_ID}" \
  -d "client_secret=${CLIENT_SECRET}" \
  -d "refresh_token=${REFRESH_TOKEN}"
```

